-- title:
--   Smallness of H¹_{fppf}(Specℤ,G[n])
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and an object $N$ of `JZeroNeronIdentityComponent p`, that is, a bundle consisting of a scheme $N.G$ in universe $0$ with a structure morphism $N.g : N.G \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathbf Z$, a commutative relative group law on $N.g$ (functorial multiplication, unit and inverse on $T$-points over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbf Z$), a bijection of $\mathrm{JZero}\,p$ — the degree-zero Picard group over $\overline{\mathbf Q}$ of the modular function field of level $p$ — with the $\overline{\mathbf Q}$-points of $N.g$ compatible with addition, the Galois action and the Hecke operators, the assumptions that $N.g$ is smooth, separated, locally of finite type, quasi-compact and surjective with preconnected fibres, flatness and surjectivity of multiplication by each positive integer, and a finite-index condition on the subgroup generated by the global sections. Let $\mathcal G$ be a sheaf of abelian groups (in `Ab.{1}`) on the small fppf site of $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbf Z$, whose objects are $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbf Z$-schemes whose structure morphism is flat and locally of finite presentation. Assume given, for every object $U$ of this site, a bijection $e_U$ from the sections $\mathcal G(U)$ to the set of morphisms $U \to N.G$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbf Z$, i.e. pairs $(\varphi : U \to N.G)$ with $\varphi$ followed by $N.g$ equal to the structure morphism of $U$; and assume (hypothesis `he`) that these bijections are compatible with restriction, in the sense that for every morphism $k : U \to V$ of the site and every $s \in \mathcal G(V)$, $e_U$ of the restriction of $s$ along $k$ equals the underlying scheme morphism of $k$ followed by $e_V(s)$. Then for every integer $n$, the first fppf cohomology group of the kernel of $n \cdot \mathrm{id}_{\mathcal G}$ on $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbf Z$ is a small type, i.e. is in bijection with a type in `Type 0`.
--
--   The statement records that $H^1_{\mathrm{fppf}}(\operatorname{Spec}\mathbf Z, \mathcal G[n])$ for the $n$-torsion of the points sheaf of the Néron identity component of $J_0(p)$ is small, a universe-level prerequisite (weaker than the classical finiteness of this group) for working with the fppf Kummer sequence on $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbf Z$ in universe $0$. It is used in the construction of the primary torsion data attached to $J_0(p)$ and in the accompanying non-emptiness statement.
-- source:
